<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anglesey</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ynys Môn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anglesey, in Welsh Ynys Môn and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Mona, is the largest island off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, separated from the mainland by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menai-strait">Menai Strait</a>. A centre of the druids, it was attacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 60 CE and conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Agricola</a> in 77, and its fertile farmland later made it the granary and royal heartland of the kingdom of Gwynedd. Edward I built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaumaris-castle">Beaumaris Castle</a> there after his conquest of Wales.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anglesey.jpg" alt="Anglesey" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Buck · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_from_the_Anglesey_Coastal_Path_(geograph_6222502).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Anglesey lies off the north-west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. At 676 square kilometres it is the largest island in Wales and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a> and the seventh largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menai-strait">Menai Strait</a>, about 250 metres wide at its narrowest, separates it from the mainland. It is low-lying, with low hills across the north, of which Holyhead Mountain, at 220 metres, is the highest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Holy Island</a> lies to its west and belongs to the same county. Large parts of the coast are an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/area-of-outstanding-natural-beauty">Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty</a>, and cliffs such as South Stack hold seabird colonies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Settlement began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> period. One of the first villages in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> was built at Llanfaethlu from about 4000 BCE, and the burial chamber of Bryn Celli Ddu began as a henge enclosure around 3000 BCE. Anglesey has long been associated with the druids. In 60 CE the Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-suetonius-paulinus">Gaius Suetonius Paulinus</a>, determined to break their power, attacked the island with an amphibious Batavian vanguard and destroyed the shrine and the sacred groves, but news of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a>'s revolt made him withdraw before he had consolidated the conquest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Gnaeus Julius Agricola</a> finally brought the island into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, and in the Roman period it was notable for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mining.</p><p>The medieval House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberffraw">Aberffraw</a>, which ruled the Kingdom of Gwynedd until 1283, originated on Anglesey and kept courts at Aberffraw and Rhosyr. After Edward I conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaumaris-castle">Beaumaris Castle</a> was built at the island's south-eastern corner. In the nineteenth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menai-strait">Menai Strait</a> was bridged by Thomas Telford's Menai Suspension Bridge of 1826 and by the Britannia Bridge, originally designed by Robert Stephenson in 1850.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holyhead">Holyhead</a>, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Holy Island</a>, is the largest town and a ferry port for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, and Llangefni is the administrative centre. Anglesey is regarded as a heartland of the Welsh language. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaumaris-castle">Beaumaris Castle</a> is part of the Castles and Town Walls of King Edward in Gwynedd World Heritage Site. The English name may derive from the Old Norse Ǫngullsey, Hook Island, or Ǫnglisey, Ǫngli's Island; the reading Island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> has no merit.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anglesey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom’s heartland was Snowdonia, Eryri, a natural fortress of mountains, together with the fertile island of <b>Anglesey</b>, Môn, whose grain fed the court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-gwynedd">Kingdom of Gwynedd · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 60 or 61, while the governor Gaius Suetonius Paulinus was attacking <b>Anglesey</b>, Boudica of the Iceni rose in revolt and destroyed Camulodunum, Londinium and Verulamium before she was defeated somewhere in the Midlands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome suppressed the druids under Tiberius and Claudius, and Tacitus describes the Roman attack in 60 or 61 CE on their stronghold of <b>Anglesey</b>, where black-robed women with torches stood among the defenders.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Promulgated at Rhuddlan Castle after the conquest of Wales, the royal ordinance divided the principality into shires, among them <b>Anglesey</b> and Flintshire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-19">19 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Anglesey</b>, the island of Mona, lay on their borders and was a centre of the druids, whose sanctuary Suetonius Paulinus attacked in 60 before Boudica's revolt recalled him.</blockquote><a href="/state/ordovices">Ordovices</a></li></ul></section>
